<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Negara Dipa</h1><p class="st-meta">1387 CE – 1495 CE (approx.) · capital Amuntai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Hindu kingdom of South Kalimantan centred at Amuntai, remembered in the Hikayat Banjar as the first royal seat of the Banjar people.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Negara Dipa is known chiefly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-banjar">Hikayat Banjar</a>, the chronicle of the later Banjar court. It tells how a merchant from Keling named Ampu Jatmika settled on the Tabalong and Balangan rivers near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amuntai">Amuntai</a> and built a temple and palace. The register's dates of 1387 to 1495 are approximate.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The chronicle says the founder did not claim kingship himself, and his son Lambung Mangkurat found a princess, Putri Junjung Buih, to marry a Javanese prince, so that the dynasty drew legitimacy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>. The remains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candi-agung">Candi Agung</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amuntai">Amuntai</a> are associated with this period. The kingdom traded gold, rattan and forest products down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barito-river">Barito</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-banjar">Hikayat Banjar</a>, the royal seat later moved downriver and the kingdom became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/negara-daha">Negara Daha</a>. Negara Daha in turn was replaced in 1526 by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banjar-sultanate">Banjar Sultanate</a>, which inherited its traditions.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1387 CE – 1495 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Maritime Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.30%;width:2.15%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1387 CE – 1495 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amuntai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Banjar Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nagara Dipa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1441&amp;focus=negara-dipa" aria-label="Negara Dipa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7880 2268 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Negara Dipa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8200 2524L8112 2721M8200 2524L8168 2746"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8112 2721h0M8168 2746h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8200 2524h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8200 2524h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Amuntai. <a href="/atlas?year=1441&amp;focus=negara-dipa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
